        "question": {
          ":question/id": "f6a13198-11f6-57ef-9cd5-f44900061ee8",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $M(x,y)=\u27e8y-{x}^{2},3x+{y}^{2},\\frac{1}{x+y}\u27e9$\n- **II.** $N(x,y)=\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+2,1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\u27e9$\n- **III.** $P(t)=\u27e8{t}^{3},\\sqrt{1-t}\\,\u27e9$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $M(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not in ${R}^{2}$).\n\n- The function $N(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $P(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ \n(not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
