        "question": {
          ":question/id": "0141a9b6-4e12-586d-84c3-9bb913fe288e",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $V(x,y)=\u27e8x{e}^{y},1-y\u27e9$\n- **II.** $U(x,y)=\u27e8\\frac{1}{x-3},\\sqrt{x-y}\u27e9$\n- **III.** $W(x,y,z)=\u27e8x,-y,-z\u27e9$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 3,\\,x-y\\ge 0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
